      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Knee pain is common among older adults but only a minority consult their doctor about it."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIM",
          "content": "To determine predictors of new episodes of consultation in primary care among older people with knee pain."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN OF STUDY",
          "content": "Population-based prospective cohort study linking baseline survey to primary care medical records."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "Three general practices in North Staffordshire, UK."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHOD",
          "content": "Subjects were 1797 people aged > or =50 years who responded to a general population survey, reported knee pain in the previous 12 months and had no record of a knee disorder consultation in the 18 months prior to the survey. The main outcome measure was a record of a knee disorder consultation in the 18 months following the survey."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The incidence of a new episode of general practice care was approximately 10% per year. Apart from chronicity (odds ratio [OR] = 1.5; 95% confidence interval [CI] = 1.1 to 2.1), measures of pain severity were not strong influences on future consultation. No social support (measured by having no partner) increased likelihood of future consultation (OR = 1.3; 95% CI = 1.0 to 1.8). Among those with chronic and severe pain, main predictors were previous experiences of health care (use of non-GP services OR = 1.8; previous knee injury OR = 1.7). Current depression reduced likelihood of consulting about the knee problem (OR = 0.6; 95% CI = 0.3 to 0.9)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Knee pain is common in the older population but a minority consult their doctor about it. Severity of pain and disability is not a strong influence on consultation. For those more severely affected, depression may act as a barrier to healthcare use."
        }
      ]},
